window.PLD=window.PLD||{};PLD["R-tx-2024-08-06"]={"CS-040-24":"Resolution No. CS-040-24\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nNIAGARA COUNTY YOUTH BUREAU - NEW YORK STATE AID APPLICATION\nWHEREAS, the County of Niagara has been designated as eligible to receive State Aid for 2024-25 youth programs tb-ough its Youth Bureau from the New York State Office of Children and Family Services in the amount of $251,016, $48,908, $103,337, $122,106, and\nWHEREAS, the Comprehensive Planning Law mandates the Niagara County Youth Bureau must apply for such funds, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review the documents for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Director of the County Youth Bureau be, and hereby is, authorized to make application for the following program funds: Youth Development Program $251,016 Youth Sports and Education Opportunity $ 48,908 Youth Team Sports $103,337 Runaway and Homeless Youth $122,106 TOTAL $525,367 and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute any and all documents relating to the application, subject to the review by the County Manager and the approval of the County Attorney.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, Hill.","CS-041-24":"Resolution No. CS-041-24\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nNCCC FY 2024-2025 MAINTENANCE AND OPERATING BUDGET\nWHEREAS, the Administration Committee and the Community Services Committee, which are also the Budget Review Committees, have had under consideration the Niagara County Community College Board of Trustees recommended budget for the conduct of the Niagara County Community College fiscal year commencing September 1, 2024 and ending August 31, 2025,and\nWHEREAS, the said budget has had careful consideration by the Administration Committee, the Community Services Committee, the Budget Office, and the County Manager, and\nWHEREAS, a public hearing was held on the 6 day of August, 2024 with regard to the tentative budget for the College, now, therefore be it\nRESOLVED, that the foregoing tentative Niagara County Community College budget for the fiscal year 2024-25, beginning September 1, 2024 and ending August 31, 2025, is hereby approved and adopted with any amendments as heretofore approved this date by this legislature and which results in a total figure of $48J60,176 with the Sponsor's contribution from taxes of $ 8,971,000.\nMoved by Gooch, seconded by Bradt.\nAdopted, 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, Hill.","CS-042-24":"Resolution No. CS-042-24\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nNIAGARA COUNTY EMPLOYMENT \u00ab& TRAINING DEPARTMENT BUDGET MODIFICATION\nACCEPT $200,000 STATE-LEVEL TRAINING AND SUPPORTIVE SERVICES FUNDING GRANT\n(SWA FISCAL INCENTIVE)\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Local Workforce Development Board (NCWDB) has been awarded $200,000 under the State-Level Training and Supportive Services Funding Grant (SWA Fiscal Incentive), and\nWHEREAS, these funds are being passed-through from Workforce Innovation and Opportunity Act (WIOA) to the New York State Department of Labor to be utilized in our local area for Adult and/or Dislocated Worker training and supportive services, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Employment & Training (NCET) Department will manage and administer these training and supportive funds as outlined in the grant Sub-recipient Agreement, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the NCWDB and NCET are authorized to accept this award, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the 2024 NCET Department Budget be modified as follows:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nCD.29.6291.000 44791.00 WIOA Revenue $ 200,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nCD.29.6291.000 74500.01 Contractual Expenses $ 200,000\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, O Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, Hill.","CS-043-24":"Resolution No. CS-043-24\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nNIAGARA COUNTY EMPLOYMENT & TRAINING DEPARTMENT BUDGET MODIFICATION\nINCREASE IN 2024 SUMMER YOUTH EMPLOYMENT PROGRAM (SYEP) FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Workforce Development Board (NCWDB) has been selected by the Niagara County Department of Social Services to administer the 2024 New York State Summer Youth Employment Program (SYEP) funded by the Office of Temporary and Disability Assistance (OTDA), and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Employment & Training (NCET) Department performs the operation and management of this annual summer program, and\nWHEREAS, the NCET 2024 budget currently reflects an estimated amount of $480,000 based upon the prior year's funding, and the actual 2024 award is $554,402, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the NCWDB and NCET are authorized to accept this award and operate the Sumer Youth Employment Program, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the 2024 NCET Department Budget be modified as follows:\nINCREASE REVENUE:\nCD.29.6291.000 44089.02 Other TANF Revenue $ 74,402\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nCD .29.6291.000 74500.01 Contractual Expenses $ 74,402\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, Hill.","CS-044-24":"Resolution No. CS-044-24\nFrom: Community Services and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nMH - 2024 BUDGET MODIFICATION - ACCEPT NEW YORK STATE OFFICE OF\nMENTAL HEALTH FUNDING FOR SPECTRUM HUMAN SERVICES\nWHEREAS, Niagara County Department of Mental Health and Substance Abuse Services receives funding from the New York State Office of Mental Health (OMH) for the oversight and provision of mental health, alcohol and substance abuse, and developmental disabilities services in Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, the New York State Office of Mental Health has given allocation of State Aid funding to Spectrum Human Services to enhance rehabilitation and employment services through Assertive Community Treatment (ACT) to support one (1) FTE Individualized Placement Services (IPS) Employment Specialist, now, therefore,\nbe it\nRESOLVED, that the following 2024 budget modifications be effectuated to the department and contract agency budget and the department is given authorization to accept New York State Office of Mental Health funding effective August 6, 2024 and engage in the process to carry out the necessary agreements between the County and Spectrum Human Services, subject to the approval of the County Attorney's Office: INCREASE REVENUES: CM.21.4322.415.43490.01 Community Support Services Programs $ 71,857 INCREASE APPROPRIATIONS: CM.21.4322.415.74500.01 Community Support Services Programs $ 71,857\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent ~- Andres, Hill.","CSS-064-24":"Resolution No. CSS-064-24\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nACCEPT AID TO CRIME LABORATOmES GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has been awarded an Aid to Crime Laboratories Grant from the New York State Division of Criminal Justice Services in the amount of $191,053 for the performance period July 1, 2024 through June 30, 2025,and\nWHEREAS, the funds are used for expenses of crime laboratories for accreditation, training, capacity enhancement and lab related services to maintain the quality and reliability of forensic services which the Niagara County Sheriffs Office Forensic Lab provides to Niagara County agencies, and\nWHEREAS, this grant is an additional year of funding for an existing grant and is included in the 2024 budget and will be built into the 2025 budget, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Aid to Crime Laboratories Grant may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, O Noes, 2 Absent ~~ Andres, Hill.","CSS-065-24":"Resolution No. CSS-065-24\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFFS OFFICE\nACCEPT OFFICE OF VICTIM SERVICES GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed Resolution #CSS-055-22 on July 25, 2022 to accept a multiple year grant from from the New York State Office of Victim Services for the term of October 1, 2022 through September 30, 2025, and\nWHEREAS, the grant award for October 1, 2024 through September 30, 2025 is $302,826.18 with the match requirement waived for this contract year, and\nWHEREAS, this grant funds the Niagara County Sheriffs Office Victim Assistance Unit with the personnel and services needed to provide legal, financial, safety and support services and referrals to Niagara County residents who are victims of crimes, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Office of Victim Services grant may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, O Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, Hill.","CSS-066-24":"Resolution No. CSS-066-24\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nACCEPT STOP VIOLENCE AGAINST WOMEN ACT FORMULA GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has been notified by the New York State Division of Criminal Justice Services that it has been awarded $50,000 under period 4 of the STOP Violence Against Women Act (VAWA) Formula Grant, and\nWHEREAS, the STOP VAWA grant funds are used to help fund a full time Crime Victim Advocate dedicated to Domestic Violence cases to help Domestic Violence victims throughout Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, the STOP VAWA Grant is a multiple year grant for a total of $250,000 from January 1, 2024 through December 31, 2025, and\nWHEREAS, the current performance period for the grant is January 1, 2024 through December 31, 2024, and\nWHEREAS, the funds are already in the 2024 budget, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the STOP Violence Against Women Act Formula Grant may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent ~- Andres, Hill.","CSS-067-24":"Resolution No. CSS-067-24\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nAMENDMENT OF SECURUS CONTRACT FOR INMATE COMMUNICATIONS SERVICES\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed Resolution CSS-033-21 on March 23, 2021 to enter into a contract with Secums Technologies, LLC for inmate communication services at the Niagara County Jail, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed Resolution CSS-041-22 on June 21, 2022 to extend the contract until October 14, 2025 with the addition of a full time onsite administrator for the Secums System at no charge to the county, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed Resolution CSS-048-22 on August 2, 2022 to amend the Secums Contract to add Video Relay Services (VRS) at a cost of $2,00o per year to the services available to incarcerated individuals at the Niagara County Jail through the Securus System to allow deaf and hard-of\" hearing incarcerated individuals the ability to communicate via a videoconferencing system which is within ADA Compliance, and\nWHEREAS, the Securus contract needs to be amended to delete the $2,000 annual cost to the County for the VRS System which will continue to be provided to the incarcerated individuals with the cost being absorbed by Securus Technologies, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the amendment to the Securus Technologies contract may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, O Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, Hill.","CSS-068-24":"Resolution No. CSS-068-24\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nBUDGET MODIFICATION - ACCEPT FY2020 EDWARD BYRNE MEMORIAL JUSTICE\nASSISTANCE LAW ENFORCEMENT EQUIPMENT GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has been notified by the New York State Division of Criminal Justice Services that it is the recipient of a grant under the New York State FY2020 Edward Byrne Memorial Justice Assistance Law Enforcement Equipment Grant in the amount of $602,000, and\nWHEREAS, this grant will allow the Sheriffs Office to purchase needed law enforcement equipment including but not limited to equipment to update the Mobile Command Center, a large monitor for the Briefing Room, Information Technology equipment and portable radios, and\nWHEREAS, the 2024 budget needs to be revised in order to purchase the needed equipment, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney review, the New York State FY2020 Edward Byrne Memorial Justice Assistance Law Enforcement Equipment Grant may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nFNCREASE ESTIMATED REVENUE:\nA.17.3645.000 44305.02 Civil Defense Homeland Security $602,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3645.000 72100.05 Computer Equipment $317,000\nA.17.3645.000 72100.15 Communications Equipment $280,000\nA.17.3645.000 72100.21 Law Enforcement Equipment $ 5,000\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, O Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, Hill.","CSS-069-24":"Resolution No. CSS-069-24\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nBUDGET MODIFICATION ~ ACCEPT GUN INVOLVED VIOLENCE ELIMINATION GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has been notified by the New York State Division of Criminal Justice Services that it has been awarded a grant under the Gun Involved Violence Elimination (GIVE) Grant in the amount of $255,250 for the performance period July 1, 2024 through June 30, 2025, and\nWHEREAS, the goal of the GIVE Initiative is the elimination of shootings and homicides, or violent crime where applicable, through the integrated use of evidence-based strategies that are incorporated into the four core elements of GIVE: People, Places, Alignment and Engagement, and\nWHEREAS, the GIVE Grant is a partnership with the Niagara County District Attorney's Office and the Niagara Falls Police Department to benefit Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, the GIVE grant funds a Correction Officer position #10442 for a Corrections Intelligence Officer, travel for DCJS sponsored training and conferences, overtime for additional law enforcement presence in Hot Spot policing and Community/Youth engagement with the Niagara Falls Police Department and funds for a Firearms Consultant for verification work in the NCSO Forensic Laboratory, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review the 2024-2025 Gun Involved Violence Elimination Initiative Grant may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE ESTIMATED REVENUE:\nA.17.3110.000 43389.13 Other Public Safety Crime Prevention $119,000\nA.17.3150.000 43389.13 Other Public Safety Crime Prevention $ 1,000\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3110.000 71050.00 Overtime Expense $ 99,000\nA.17.31IO.OOO 74500.01 Contractual Expenses $ 20,000\nA.17.3150.000 74600.03 Training and Education $ 1,000\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, Hill.","CSS-070-24":"Resolution No. CSS-070-24\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nBUDGET MODIFICATION - ACCEPT REVENUE FROM SALE OF K9 VEHICLE\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office purchased a 2012 Chevrolet Tahoe for the Canine Division which was acquired on May 10, 2012 under a New York State Division of Homeland Security and Emergency Services Explosive Detection Canine Team Grant, and\nWHEREAS, the 2012 Chevrolet Tahoe was sold by Niagara County at auction for $1,975.00, and\nWHEREAS, the proceeds from the sale of the vehicle purchased under the grant must be used to purchase equipment which would be a qualifying purchase under the Explosive Detection Canine Team Grant, and\nWHEREAS, the revenue and corresponding expense must be added to the 2024 budget so that the Niagara County Sheriffs Office is able to comply with the grant guidelines, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated:\nINCREASE ESTIMATED REVENUE:\nA.17.31IO.OOO 42650.00 Sale of Scrap and Excess Materials Revenue $ 1,975\nINCREASE APPROPRIATIONS:\nA.17.3UO.OOO 74550.32 Programs Special Task Force $ 1,975\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, O Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, Hill.","CSS-071-24":"Resolution No. CSS-071-24\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nEXTEND CONTRACT FOR UNIFORM ACCESSORIES\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Purchasing Office issued Sealed Bid 2020-36 Deputy and Corrections Accessories for Niagara County Sheriffs Office which was awarded to United Uniform Company, LLC on August 2, 2020, and\nWHEREAS, the term of the award was for three (3) years with the option for two (2) one (1) year extensions, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed Resolution #CSS-077-23 on August 1, 2023 to exercise the first one year extension to extend the contract until August 1,2024,and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office and United Uniform Company, LLC wish to exercise the second one (1) year extension, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the award for Deputy and Corrections Accessories for Niagara County Sheriffs Office be extended for one year until August 1, 2025.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, O Noes, 2 Absentm Andres, Hill.","CSS-072-24":"Resolution No. CSS-072-24\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nEXTEND CONTRACT FOR UNIFORMS\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Purchasing Office issued Sealed Bid 2020-35 Deputy, Corrections and Emergency Services Police Uniforms which was awarded to Galls LLC on July 28,2020,and\nWHEREAS, the term of the award was from 7/28/20 - 7/27/23 with the option for two (2) one (1) year extensions, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed Resolution CSS-076-23 on August 1, 2023 to exercise the first one year extension and extend the contract until 7/27/24, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office and Galls LLC wish to exercise the second one(l) year extension with some price increases which are supported by documentation from manufacturers and have been reviewed and accepted by Niagara County Purchasing, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the award for Deputy, Corrections and Emergency Services Police Uniforms be extended for one year until July 27, 2025.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, Hill.","CSS-073-24":"Resolution No. CSS-073-24\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nMCP ACQUISITION STANDARDIZATION FOR RADIO TOWER DESIGN,\nPROCUREMENT AND CONSTRUCTION SERVICES\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed Resolution #CSS-073\"18 on 9/18/18 to enter into a job specific contract with MCP Acquisition DBA Mission Critical Partners LLC (\"Mission Critical\") to review, examine and negotiate the Motorola Proposal for a Countywide Public Safety Radio Project, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed Resolution #CSS-007-19 on 2/19/19 to waive the Request for Proposal and enter into a professional services contract with Mission Critical to manage the Radio System Two Site Implementation Project, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed Resolution #CSS-004-20 on 1/21/20 to waive the Request for Proposal and enter into a professional services contract with Mission Critical to technically review, procurement review, project management, contract negotiations and implementation of the Radio System Six Site Project, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has identified the need for services for radio tower design, procurement and construction services for a new communications tower in Niagara County, and\nWHEREAS, Mission Critical has been managing the implementation of the construction and integration of new tower sites in the Niagara County Public Safety Communications System since 2018 which gives the firm unique expertise and system knowledge, and\nWHEREAS, under Niagara County's Purchasing Guidelines, the Niagara County Legislature and the requisite committee(s) have the discretion to waive the Requests for Proposal for a professional services contract, and\nWHEREAS, Mission Critical has prepared a proposal for Radio Tower Design, Procurement, and Construction Services for $149,468 which will be paid for out of Statewide Interoperable Communications Grants, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the County finds that the services to be provided by Mission Critical consist of \"professional services\" and further waives the RFP requirement and/or requirement that additional cost proposals be sought or obtained, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney review and approval of the contract, that the Mission Critical contract for General Consulting may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, Hill.","CSS-074-24":"Resolution No. CSS-074-24\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHEmFF'S OFFICE\nSOLE SOURCE STANDARDIZATION OF NOMAD GCS FOR\nMOBILE COMMAND CENTER UPDATE\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office purchased a Mobile Command Center in 2012 which has a Nomad Total Command Technology System and components which are due to be refreshed with a newer version, and\nWHEREAS, it is important to keep the Mobile Command Center updated due to its critical communications function in emergency situations, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has secured grant funding for the project to update the Nomad Total Command Technology System to the current 4.0 iteration and to update necessary components, and\nWHEREAS, the existing Nomad Total Command System is proprietary to NOMAD GCS and is the only vehicle automation system on the market that Is NIST 800-171 compliant for security, with the quote for the upgrade in the amount of $261,772.30, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that for reasons of efficiency and economy, pursuant to General Municipal Law 103(5), there is a need for standardization for the Niagara County Sheriffs Office in the purchase of the upgrade and refresh of the Nomad Total Command System and the Niagara County Sheriffs Office Mobile Command Center to the 4.0 iteration, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that it is hereby determined that NOMAD GCS is the sole source vendor for the upgrade and refresh of the Nomad Total Command System and the Niagara County Sheriffs Office Mobile Command Center to the 4.0 iteration, and that the Sheriffs Office is hereby authorized to purchase the upgrade and refresh of the Nomad Total Command System and the Niagara County Sheriffs Office Mobile Command Center to the 4.0 iteration directly from NOMAD GCS without the need to solicit for alternate proposals, such purchase to be made in consultation with the Purchasing Department and in accordance with budgetary procedures.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, O Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, Hill.","CSS-075-24":"Resolution No. CSS-075-24\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFF'S OFFICE\nSCHOOL RESOURCE OFFICER AGREEMENT WITH NEWFANE CENTRAL SCHOOL DISTRICT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has been in an agreement with the Newfane Central School District to provide a Deputy Sheriff as a School Resource Office in exchange for compensation from the School District, and\nWHEREAS, the term of the previous agreement was September 1 , 2023 through August 3 1,2024,and\nWHEREAS, school safety remains a high priority for Niagara County and the Newfane Central School District, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office and the Newfane Central School District would like to enter into a multiple year contract for School Resource Officer services starting with the new school year in September 2024, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Newfane Central School District School Resource Officer Agreement may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent ~ Andres, Hill.","CSS-076-24":"Resolution No. CSS-076-24\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nNIAGARA COUNTY SHERIFFS OFFICE\nLABVANTAGE STANDARDIZATION/SOLE SOURCE OF JBOSS APPLICATION SERVER AND\nBARTENDER ENTERPRISE AUTOMATION EDITION COMPATIBLE WITH LABVANTAGE\nLABORATORY INFORMATION MANAGEMENT SYSTEM\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature passed Resolution # CSS-124-23 on November 14, 2023 approving the purchase, installation, training and service of a Laboratory Information System from LabVantage Solutions Inc., for the Niagara County Sheriffs Office Forensic Laboratory, and\nWHEREAS, the LabVantage Laboratory Information Management System (LIMS) also needs the JBOSS Application Server and BarTender Enterprise Automation Edition which are compatible with the system in order to perform all of the needed functions of the LIMS to increase efficiency in the Forensic Laboratory, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Sheriffs Office has made exhaustive attempts to identify alternative sources of the JBOSS Application Server and BarTender Enterprise Automation Edition which are $6,162 through Lab Vantage Solutions but have been unsuccessful due to the custom part numbers of the product, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that for reasons of efficiency and economy, pursuant to General Municipal Law 103(5), there is a need for standardization for the Niagara County Sheriffs Office in the purchase of the JBOSS Application Server and BarTender Enterprise Automation Edition for the LabVantage Laboratory Information Management System, and that the purchase of such be standardized to LABVANTAGE Solutions Inc., services, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that it is hereby determined that LabVantage Solutions Inc is the sole source for the JBOSS Application Server and BarTender Enterprise Automation Edition compatible with the Lab Vantage Laboratory Information Management System, and that the Sheriffs Office is hereby authorized to purchase the JBOSS Application Server and BarTender Enterprise Automation Edition compatible with the LabVantage Laboratory Information Management System from LabVantage Solutions Inc. without the need to solicit for alternate proposals or sealed bids, such purchases to be made in consultation with the Purchasing Department and in accordance with budgetary procedures.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent ~ Andres, Hill.","CSS-077-24":"Resolution No. CSS-077-24\nFrom; Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nPROBATION RENEWAL OF GIVE GRANT\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Probation Department has been notified by the New York State Division of Criminal Justice Services that the Gun Involved Violence Elimination (GIVE) grant has been renewed for the period July 1, 2024 - June 30, 2025, and\nWHEREAS, the grant is being renewed in the amount of $375,267 and said funds are used to pay for two Probation Officer positions and a .5 FTE Field Intelligence Officer position within the Probation Department, in addition to funding field activities, GPS monitoring of offenders, and staff training, and\nWHEREAS, the GIVE funds have been budgeted in the 2024 budget, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the grant agreement, the County Attorney will review the agreement for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute said agreement.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, Hill.","CSS-078-24":"Resolution No. CSS-078-24\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nPROBATION ACCEPT NIAGARA COUNTY PRETRIAL SERVICES AWARD\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Probation Department has been notified by the New York State Division of Criminal Justice Services of an award of funding to offset the costs associated with the provision of certified pretrial services in the amount of $521,493, and\nWHEREAS, funding will be utilized by the Probation Department for pretrial services including but not limited to, screening, assessment, supervision, and reporting as provided in the enacted NYS budget, and.\nWHEREAS, the grant award revenue and associated expense will be included in the Probation Department s 2025 requested budget, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the grant agreement, the County Attorney will review the agreement for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute said agreement.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, Hill.","CSS-079-24":"Resolution No. CSS-079-24\nFrom; Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nEMERGENCY SERVICES - AWARD OF BID - COMMUNITY PREPAREDNESS TRAINING\nWHEREAS, Niagara County has prepared specifications and the Niagara County Purchasing Department has advertised for the Bid RFP 2024-37 in search of a proven experienced and qualified firm capable of performing community preparedness training focused on creating a disaster resilient community, and\nWHEREAS, only one bid response was received from Niagara University in the amount of $90,000 over a three-year period, and\nWHEREAS) Niagara University s Border Community SERVICE (Special Emergency Response Volunteer Initiative for Community Empowerment) meets and exceeds all the qualifications of the RFP including over 15 years of direct experience providing community emergency preparedness outreach, education and training, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara University has worked closely with CERT and other community volunteers to provide valuable assistance for emergency services like Point of Dispensing (POD) drills with the County's Public Health Planning and Emergency Preparedness Office, the County's Healthy Neighborhoods program, Childhood Lead Poisoning Primary Prevention Program to promote programming and further educate residents, and has hosted numerous Stop the Bleed trainings, and\nWHEREAS, funding is included in the SHSP Homeland Security Budget for professional and technical services to assist the County in completing intended community preparedness initiatives, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, the RFP 2024-37 Community Preparedness Training Services for Niagara County Emergency Services be awarded to Niagara University, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Community Preparedness Training Services for Niagara County Emergency Services contract with Niagara University may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, O Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, Hill.","CSS-080-24":"Resolution No. CSS-080-24\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nEMERGENCY SERVICES - AMBULANCE DIVISION\nAWARD OF BID - MEDICAL DIRECTOR\nWHEREAS, Niagara County has prepared specifications and the Niagara County Purchasing Department has advertised for the Bid 2024-38 in search for pre-qualified service providers for Professional Services for an EMS Medical Director, and\nWHEREAS, only one bid response was received from UB|MD for a two-year agreement, $35,000 for year one and $37,000 for year two, and\nWHEREAS, funds are available in the Emergency Services/County Ambulance Services budget (A.19.4540),\nnow, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, the RFP 2024-38 EMS Medical Director for Niagara County Emergency Medical Services be awarded to UB]MD Emergency Medicine EMS Division, 462 Grider Street, Buffalo, NY 14215, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the EMS Medical Director for Niagara County Emergency Medical Services contract with Dr. John McNamara from UB|MD may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, O Noes, 2 Absent ~- Andres, Hill.","CSS-081-24":"Resolution No. CSS-081-24\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nEMERGENCY SERVICES - MOU WITH SUNY NIAGARA TO UTILIZE NIAGARA COUNTY\nPUBLIC SAFETY BUILDING FOR EMERGENCY MEDICAL TECHNICIAN (EMT) TRAINING\nWHEREAS, emergency medical response personnel are required to be certified and maintain certification to meet state and regional guidelines, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Public Safety Training Facility has adequate classroom space, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County Community College (SUNY Niagara) has a desire to utilize said classroom space to reach a greater number of community residents by offering Emergency Medical Technician (EMT) training,\nnow, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Legislature determines that the EMT training offered through SUNY Niagara be satisfactory to meet state and regional guidelines for maintaining the certifications of our County EMS personnel, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, said MOU with SUNY Niagara for EMT training may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, Hill.","CSS-082-24":"Resolution No. CSS-082-24\nFrom: Community Safety & Security and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nEMERGENCY SERVICES\nINTER-MUNICIPAL AMBULANCE TRANSFER AGREEMENT\nWHEREAS, Niagara County Department of Emergency Services currently possesses a 2010 Ford EC4 Road Rescue, ambulance, previously used during the initial operational start to the county ambulance services which the county wishes to declare as surplus property, and\nWHEREAS, the Orleans County Department of Emergency Management has a need for an ambulance matching the above description for use by the Orleans County EMS division in the performance of their duties, and\nWHEREAS, an Inter-Municipal Agreement between the County of Niagara and the Orleans County Department of Emergency Management has been drafted by the County Attorney s Office for a transfer of aforementioned 2010 Ford EC4 Road Rescue, ambulance, new therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the County of Niagara declares the 2010 Ford EC4 Road Rescue, ambulance, to be surplus property and authorizes an agreement with the Orleans County Department of Emergency Management to transfer ownership of the 2010 Ford EC4 Road Rescue, ambulance, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature be, and hereby is, authorized to execute the agreement with Orleans County Department of Emergency Management.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, Hill.","ED-015-24":"Resolution No. ED-015-24\nFrom: Economic Development and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nPOSITION APPOINTMENT DEPUTY COMMISSIONER OF\nNIAGARA COUNTY DEPARTMENT OF ECONOMIC DEVELOPMENT\nWHEREAS, under the Local Law establishing an Administration Code for the County of Niagara, the County Manager shall make appointments for the position of Deputy Commissioner of Economic Development subject to the confirmation of the County Legislature, and\nWHEREAS, resolution AD-014-18 named Andrea Klyczek Deputy Commissioner of Economic Development, and\nWHEREAS, Niagara County and the Niagara County Industrial Development Agency entered into a shared services agreement on June 19, 2018,and\nWHEREAS, on June 12, 2024 the Niagara County Industrial Development Agency unanimously voted to promote Andrea Klyczek to the position of Executive Director, and\nWHEREAS, subject to the NCIDA by-Laws adopted on August 22, 1972 and last amended on April 20, 1995 provides that the Agency may employ an Executive Director, and\nWHEREAS, pursuant to section 858(6) of the Act, the Agency is authorized and empowered, with consent of the County, to use agents and employees of the County, paying the County its agreed proportion of the compensation or costs, and\nWHEREAS, the Agency wishes to contract with the County for services of Andrea Klyczek, Deputy Commissioner of the Niagara County Economic Development to act as the Executive Director of the Agency,\nnow, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, the County shall enter into an agreement for the services of the Deputy Commissioner of Economic Development, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Deputy Commissioner of Economic Development shared services agreement may be executed by the Chairman of the Niagara County Legislature pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent ~ Andres, Hill.","ED-016-24":"Resolution No. ED-016-24\nFrom: Economic Development and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nCITY OF NIAGARA FALLS REQUEST FOR WAIVER OF\nCOUNTY TAXES, INTEREST AND FEES 502 NIAGARA STREET\nWHEREAS, In the 2007 In Rem Tax Foreclosure brought by the City of Niagara Falls in 2011, the City obtained ajudgment on 502 Niagara Street (SBL 159.29-3-38), and\nWHEREAS, due to concerns about hazardous material liability (a gas station and a dry-cleaning facility previously on said parcel) the City never took title, and\nWHEREAS, a Phase I study was completed on the property in 2019, where environmental concerns were identified, and\nWHEREAS, due to the fact that title never passed to the City, the parcel continued to accrue taxes, and\nWHEREAS, the City has a proposal from TM Montante Development for a major development on the site, and the City has agreed to transfer the site for $1, and\nWHEREAS, on June 26, 2024 the Niagara Falls City Council passed a resolution waiving the accrued back taxes, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, the County of Niagara agrees and consents to forego collection of unpaid county property taxes, interest and fees up to and including 2024 for 502 Niagara Street, Niagara Falls, New York, be it further\nRESOLVED, in the event an agreement for acquisition of the respective properties, by TM Montante, is not achieved, the County of Niagara will obtain its unpaid property taxes on the property after a foreclosure auction pursuant to the formula provided by law, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that if the project proposed by TM Montante Development at 502 Niagara Street is not completed as identified in the agreement, the County of Niagara will obtain its unpaid property taxes, interest and fees on said property.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, Hill.","ED-017-24":"Resolution No. ED-017-24\nFrom: Economic Development Committee.\nDated: August 6,2024\nFIXING DATE AND NOTICE FOR THE SECOND PUBLIC HEAmNG\nFOR GRIFFON BREWING INC. COMMUNITY DEVELOPMENT BLOCK GRANT\nWHEREAS, the County Legislature wishes to hear public comments relating to the recently completed Community Development Block Grant (\"CDBG\") project #806ED1001-21 supporting the grant funding provided to Griffon Brewing Inc. at 200 Lockport Street within the Village of Youngstown, New York, and\nWHEREAS, the CDBG program is administered by the New York State Office of Community Renewal\n(\"OCR\"), and provides resources to eligible local governments for housing, economic development, public facilities, public infrastructure, and planning activities, with the principal purpose of benefitting low/moderate income persons, and\nWHEREAS, OCR requires grant recipients to hold a public hearing to provide information to the public and to consider citizen comments regarding CDBG funded projects to help make determinations for the future use of CDBG funds, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature hereby calls for a Public Hearing whereat all interested parties shall be heard, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Public Hearing shall be held at the Legislative Chambers, Courthouse, Lockport, New York on the 17th day of September, 2024, at 5:50 p.m., and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the Clerk of the Legislature at least eight (8) days' notice of such hearing shall post a notice upon the County Legislature website, the bulletin boards in the Courthouse at Lockport, and the Civic Building at Niagara Falls, and shall publish such notice once in the Lockport Union Sun & Journal, and the Niagara Gazette.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, Hill.","IF-127-24":"Resolution No. IF-127-24\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nCAPITAL PROJECT H669 CLOSEOUT\nWATER DISTRICT\nWHEREAS, the following capital project has been completed and has an account balance remaining that needs to be transferred back to the original funding sources, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that project H669 m Water Chlorine System Upgrades be closed and the following budget modifications be effectuated: DECREASE ESTIMATED REVENUES: H669.31.8397.000 45031.00 Interftmd Transfers from Operating $36,370.16 DECREASE APPROPRIATIONS: H669.31.8397.000 72100.27 Water - Chlorme System Upgrades $36,370.16 and, be it further\nRESOLVED, that the unspent funds of $36,370.16 be returned to the original funding source as follows: Water Fund Operating: $36,370.16\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, O Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, Hill.","IF-128-24":"Resolution No. IF-128-24\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nBUDGET MODIFICATION FOR LEACHATE HAULING TRUCK\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Refuse Disposal District's, 2002 Volvo Leachate Hauling Truck, requires extensive and necessary repairs to the transmission and tank body, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Refuse Disposal District is required to remove and transport landfill leachate offsite for treatment, and\nWHEREAS, the 5,000 gallon leachate hauling tank integrity is compromised and in need of replacement, and\nWHEREAS, funds are available in fund balance to replace the leachate hauling truck, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be approved:\nINCREASE APPROPRIATED FUND BALANCE\nEL 40599.00 Appropriated Fund Balance $250,000.00\nINCREASE ANTICIPATED EXPENSES:\nEL.30.8160.807.72100.10 Heavy Equipment $250,000.00\nand be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the required documents may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, O Noes, 2 Absent ~ Andres, Hill.","IF-129-24":"Resolution No. IF-129-24\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nAGREEMENT BETWEEN THE COUNTY OF NIAGARA AND DISC GOLF 716\nWHEREAS, Disc Golf 716 has requested that the County of Niagara grant them exclusive rights to operate a Disc Golf Tournament in an area situated on the County owned property at Royalton Ravine Park, and\nWHEREAS, this program benefits the residents of Niagara County as a whole, and\nWHEREAS, the event coordinator will provide all insurances required by the Risk Management Office, and agree to cover all extraordinary expenses associated with the events, and\nWHEREAS, it is the desire of Disc Golf 716 to hold a tournament September 8, 2024 at Royalton Ravine Park, and\nWHEREAS, it is the desire of Niagara County to enter into a formal agreement with the Disc Golf 716 for this tournament, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the agreement, the County Attorney will review the agreement for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore be it\nRESOLVED, that the terms and conditions of the agreement between the County of Niagara and Disc Golf 716, as appears on the proposed agreement, is hereby approved in all respects, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the agreement with the Disc Golf 716 may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, Hill.","IF-130-24":"Resolution No. IF-130-24\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nAGREEMENT BETWEEN THE COUNTY OF NIAGARA\nAND THE OLCOTT LION'S CLUB\nWHEREAS, the Olcott Lions Club has requested that the County of Niagara grant them permission to use the Lions Club Pavillion and a shelter at Krull Park for the purpose of holding The Olcott Lions Club Kindergarten Round-up, and\nWHEREAS, this event benefits the residents of the Town ofNewfane and Niagara County as a whole, and\nWHEREAS, it is the wish of the Olcott Lion's Club to hold the The Olcott Lions Club Kindergarten Round-up on August 17,2024, and\nWHEREAS, the event coordinator will provide all insurances required by the Risk Management Office, and agree to cover all extraordinary expenses associated with event, and\nWHEREAS, it is the desire of Niagara County to enter into a formal agreement with the Olcott Lion's Club, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the agreement, the County Attorney will review the agreement for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the terms and conditions of the agreement between the County of Niagara and the Olcott Lion's Club, as appears on the proposed agreement, is hereby approved in all respects, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Chairman of the Legislature is hereby authorized to execute the required documents.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, Hill.","IF-131-24":"Resolution No. IF-131-24\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nRESCIND LEASE AGREEMENT WITH IROQUOIS JOB CORPS\nACADEMY FOR THE TROTT ACCESS CENTER\nWHEREAS, on April 15, 2024 the Infrastructures and Facilities Committee recommended that the County of Niagara and Iroquois Job Corps Center enter into a lease agreement to rent space in the Trott ACCESS Center, Niagara Falls, New York, and\nWHEREAS, on April 23, 2024 the County Legislature approved the lease agreement between the County of Niagara and Iroquois Job Corp Center to rent available space at the Trott ACCESS Center, Niagara Falls, New York, and\nWHEREAS, we are requesting the rescinding of Resolution IF-075-24 since Iroquois Job Corps Center has decided not to rent the available space in the Trott ACCESS Center, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Infrastructures and Facilities Committee recommends Resolution IF-075-24, be rescinded for the lease agreement with Iroquois Job Corps Center for office space at the Trott ACCESS Center.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, O Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, Hill.","IF-132-24":"Resolution No. IF-132-24\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nLEASE AGREEMENT WITH NCCC FOR THE TROTT ACCESS CENTER\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Community College (NCCC), Sanborn, New York, is a partner in the Trott ACCESS Center, Niagara Falls, New York, and\nWHEREAS, the County provides space to NCCC for this activity, and\nWHEREAS, NCCC is able to obtain New York State Reimbursement for a portion of the costs of the space used in delivering its programs, and\nWHEREAS, NCCC needs to have a lease agreement to serve as a basis for its reimbursement claims, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Infrastructure and Facilities Committee recommends the lease agreement to be entered into with NCCC for office space at the Trott ACCESS Center, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the lease for Niagara County Community College may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, O Noes, 2 Absent ~ Andres, Hill.","IF-133-24":"Resolution No. IF-133-24\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\n225 SOUTH NIAGARA STREET, LOCKPORT, NY\nPHASE I AND II ENVIRONMENTAL SITE ASSESSMENT\nAMENDMENT NO. 1\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-010-23, dated January 17, 2023, awarded the contract for a Phase I and II Environmental Site Assessment Study and the removal of an underground diesel tank and soil testing at 225 South Niagara Street, Lockport, to C&S Engineers, 499 Col Eileen Collins Blvd., Syracuse, NY 13212, for a contract amount of $30,800, and\nWHEREAS, it is necessary to extend the contract completion date to December 31, 2026, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the contract to C & S Engineers, 499 Col Eileen Collins Blvd., Syracuse, NY 13212, be extended to December 31 , 2026, at no additional cost to the County, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Amendment may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, O Noes, 2 Absent -~ Andres, Hill.","IF-134-24":"Resolution No. IF-134-24\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6,2024\nKRULL PARK SHORELINE REHABILITATION - FINAL PAYMENT\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-145-22, dated November 15, 2022, awarded the contract for the Krull Park Shoreline Wall and Bank Reconstruction Project to 4th Generation Construction, 5650 Simmons Avenue, Niagara Falls, NY 14304, for a contract amount not to exceed $3,518,698.00, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-014-24, dated January 23, 2024, increased the contract in the amount of $65,217.41 for work to provide additional pavement resurfacing at Franklin Street, additional handrail at the rear of the band stage, and additional fencing at the pier gates, for a revised contract amount of $3,583,915.41, and\nWHEREAS, Resolution No. IF-084-24, dated April 23, 2024, increased the contract in the amount of $534,929.51 to provide labor, material, equipment, and supervision to furnish and install additional work as identified on Exhibit A and labeled as Change Order No. 2, for a revised contract amount of $4,118,844.92, and\nWHEREAS, the contract has been completed to specifications for the contract amount of $4,118,844.92, and\nWHEREAS, prior to the execution of the required documents, the County Attorney will review them for approval as to legal form, language and compliance, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that final payment is authorized to be processed to 4{ Generation Construction, 5650 Simmons Avenue, Niagara Falls, NY 14304 for a final contract amount of $4,118,844.92, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that, following the County Attorney's review, the Change Order may be executed pursuant to Niagara County Contract Policy.\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, Hill.","IF-136-24":"Resolution No. IF-136-24\nFrom: Infrastructure & Facilities and Administration Committees.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nRESOLVED, that the following budget modification be effectuated to allow the project to proceed: INCREASE ANTICIPATED REVENUE: H8XX.15.1620.000 43021.00 State Aid Court Facilities Revenue $99,850 INCREASE APPROPRIATIONS: H8XX.15.1620.000 72200.01 Building Improvements $99,850 and be it further\nRESOLVED, that the consultant services contract for the Niagara County Magistrate Hearing Room Renovation and Second Floor Redesign Project, at the Niagara County Court House, 175 Hawley Street, Lockport, NY 14094, be awarded to LaBella Associates, P.C., 300 State Street, Suite 201, Rochester, NY 14614, for a contract amount not to exceed $99,850, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that following the County Attorney's review, the Contract may be executed pursuant to the Niagara County Contract Policy.\nApproved for Submission.\nMoved by Foti, seconded by Nemi, to implement the portion of Rule 15 that pertains to voting on a resolution as opposed to referring it to committee. Carried.\nMoved by Foti, seconded by Nemi.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, O Noes, 2 Absent - Andres, Hill.","IL-111-24":"Resolution No. IL-111-24\nFrom: Legislators Richard E. Abbott, Carla L. Speranza, Anthony J. Nemi and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF BILLS ELVIS CHARITY FOUNDATION INC. THROUGH THE\nUSE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDS\nWHEREAS, the Bills Elvis Charity Foundation Inc. is locally based, and\nWHEREAS, the Bills Elvis Charity Foundation Inc. will be sponsoring an event to raise money for locally based charities, and\nWHEREAS, the Bills Elvis Charity Foundation Inc.is sponsoring an event in Lockport on September 12 2024, and\nWHEREAS, the funds will be utilized for expenses including promotions, advertising and other event expenses,\nnow, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the Bills Elvis Charity Foundation Inc. by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership as follows: Bills Elvis Charity Foundation Inc. $2,500.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, O Noes, 2 Absent ~ Andres, Hill.","IL-112-24":"Resolution No. IL-112-24\nFrom: Legislator David E. Godfrey and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF THE TOWN OF WILSON THROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY\nPARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, the Town ofWilson is home to Greenwood Cemetery, the only active cemetery in the town and\nWHEREAS, the Town ofWilsons Veterans Memorial is located at Greenwood Cemetery, and\nWHEREAS, in order to show proper respect to all area veterans, and others who call Greenwood Cemetery their final resting place, the Town ofWilson would like to purchase a cremation stand, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Program Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports the efforts of the Town of Wilson by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Fund as follows: TownofWilson $300.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, 0 Noes, 2 Absent- Andres, Hill.","IL-113-24":"Resolution No. IL-113-24\nFrom: Legislator Michael A. Hill and Economic Development Committee.\nDated: August 6, 2024\nRESOLUTION IN SUPPORT OF AMERICAN LEGION POST 1253\nTHROUGH THE USE OF COMMUNITY PARTNERSHIP FUNDING\nWHEREAS, American Legion 1253 Gasport Memorial Post was formed to recognize the sacrifice of the men and women who fought for our nation, and to support military veterans and their families, and\nWHEREAS, members of American Legion Post 1253 broke ground on a display that will incorporate a donated Bell UH-1 \"\"Huey\" helicopter, and\nWHEREAS, the helicopter display will be located on the Hartland Volunteer Fire Company property near the entrance of the town park, and\nWHEREAS, this display will act as a reminder of the brave heroes in the community that fought in the Vietnam War, and\nWHEREAS, displays such as this would not be possible without community involvement and donations, and\nWHEREAS, the Niagara County Legislature wishes to support the efforts of the American Legion Post 1253 to impress upon the residents, both young and old, the cost of defending our freedoms, now, therefore, be it\nRESOLVED, that the Niagara County Legislature authorized the allocation of funding for the Community Partnership Fund through resolution CW-001-23, and be it further\nRESOLVED, that Niagara County supports American Legion Post 1253 by appropriating monies from said Community Partnership Program Fund as follows: American Legion 1253 Gasport Memorial Post $25,000.00\nMoved by Bradt, seconded by Robins.\nAdopted. 13 Ayes, O Noes, 2 Absent ~- Andres, Hill."};
